From a6ded58e64357e53a156784a1f7786ad8835b439 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: dgabehar <dgabehar@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 25 Sep 2026 10:00:38 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] common : split-balance: fix the calibration use-after-free,
 detect busy nodes against a reference

Fleet QA round 5 (Dawn) found four problems in the per-layer calibration:

- read_model_layers freed the GGUF context before the per-layer key reads, so
  10 of 17 auto/force starts crashed (asserts in can_mul_mat/view_1d, GPFs, a
  4e15-byte alloc). An ASan build with two CPU rpc-server workers hit it on
  every start. The context is now held in a gguf_context_ptr (Dawn's fix), which
  also frees it on the early returns.
- Mixed quants made a layer kind per type layout: Qwen3.8 UD-Q4_K_XL had 53
  kinds over 3 structures, 118 s of calibration with a 780M worker. Kinds are
  now grouped by structure, timed on their most common type layout, and decode
  is scaled by the kind's mean bytes. Graphs over 50 ms are timed once per
  round (34 s -> 9 s on a slow iGPU, same predictions).
- A load lasting the whole calibration slows every round alike, so the
  first-round spread never flagged it, and the slow result was cached for 7
  days. Now each device and model keeps a build-independent reference entry:
  - a calibration more than 1.25x slower than it is logged as busy and cached
    for an hour
  - a cached entry the device now beats by more than 1.25x is re-measured
  - after a build change, a device matching its reference on the quick check
    reuses it (2.4 s start instead of a full calibration)

  The first-round spread log, which fired on idle nodes, is debug-only now.
- --split-calibrate force wrote an empty cache over the whole file; it now
  keeps the other entries. The temp file name is unique per write.

test-split-balance calibrates the CPU on attention, GDN and MoE layers through
the cache paths. An ASan run (two CPU rpc-server workers, 4 models, forced and
cached) is clean.

Assisted-by: Claude Opus 5.5
